nv30: release the resource reference in surface_del

nv30_miptree_surface_new() takes a reference on the resource it wraps:

    pipe_resource_reference(&ps->texture, pt);

and nv30_miptree_surface_del() frees the surface without dropping it. Every
render target surface therefore leaks one reference on its miptree, the
resource never reaches refcount zero, nv30_miptree_destroy() never runs, and
the texture memory is never released.

nv50_surface_destroy() in the sibling driver has the missing line, immediately
before the same FREE(). nv30 is simply missing it.

Measured on a GeForce 7600 AGP with Xorg 21.1.24 + glamor, instrumenting the
create/destroy pair: while a screensaver animated, miptree new=16782
del=14717, live climbing 1359 -> 1475 -> 1619 -> 1809 -> 2065 and never
falling, at roughly 150-250 leaked per 512 texture transfers. The transfer
path over the same window was exactly balanced (10240 mapped, 10239 unmapped,
0 MB held), which is what pointed at the surface refcount rather than the
allocator.

The user-visible effect is that the X server grows by about 38 MB/s whenever
anything animates -- the memory is only returned when X exits. On a 2 GB
machine a blank screensaver exhausts memory in roughly two minutes, and the
session dies either to the OOM killer or to a null dereference in
glamor_create_pixmap() when an allocation finally fails. Identical hardware
and X server on llvmpipe leaks 2 MB in 60 seconds.

--- a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_miptree.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_miptree.c
@@ -577,6 +577,7 @@
 static void
 nv30_miptree_surface_del(struct pipe_context *pipe, struct pipe_surface *ps)
 {
+   pipe_resource_reference(&ps->texture, NULL);
    FREE(ps);
 }
 
